  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe cold front has moved through, allowing for some relief from the \noppressive Tds we saw early this morning. Northwesterly flow \nhas brought elevated wildfire smoke back in from the Pacific \nNorthwest. This will continue to be the case throughout at least\nthe first half of the week. Models indicate that the majority \nof this smoke will remain elevated, but surface smoke isn't out \nof the question for far western Minnesota. Surface wise today, \ntemperatures have once again reached 95 or better early this \nafternoon across southeastern Minnesota and western Wisconsin \nwhere a Heat Advisory remains in effect through this evening. \nHigh temperatures will continue to run a few degrees above \nnormal in the upper 80s/low 90s with a few low chances of spotty\nafternoon showers and storms. PoPs may need to be increased \nTuesday and Wednesday afternoon, but for now any impacts look \nminimal. NBM did introduce some 15-20% PoPs for Wednesday, which\nappears reasonable.\n\nWe don't see any widespread/organized precip chances until Thursday. \nA trough moving along the top of the ridge centered over the \nsouthern Rockies looks to dig southward across the Dakotas/Minnesota \nbefore potentially evolving into a cutoff low late in the week. \nWidespread rainfall and thunderstorms are expected late Thursday \nthrough Friday. With PWATs increasing to 1.6 to 1.8\", we would \nexpect these to hopefully bring the region some much needed precip. \nQPF totals range from a quarter inch for central Minnesota to an \ninch or better to the south. This system looks to meander around the \nwestern Ohio River Valley into the weekend, which would allow our \nwestern Wisconsin counties to see continued low shower chances into \nthe weekend. Following the bulk of the rainfall Friday, temperatures \ndo fall back into the mid 80s, but this looks to be shortlived once \nagain with 90s possible early next week.",
